//! lotus/stereo_halve.rye — ALES139: the half-wave rectifier carried into stereo, ONE SHARED THRESHOLD AT ZERO, the EVEN+ODD member of the stereo NONLINEAR class's even corner. ALES85 named `halve` — y = max(x, 0),
//! keep the positive half of the wave whole and fold the negative half AWAY to silence (where the full-wave rectifier ALES84 folded it UP). Where a diode bridge makes full-wave rectification, a single diode makes
//! half-wave — it passes current one way and blocks it the other; harmonically it is the average of the dry and full-wave waves, max(x, 0) = (x + |x|) / 2, an EVEN+ODD mix carrying the fundamental plus the even
//! harmonics plus a DC term, not the pure even of |x|. Yet a Lotus master is a StereoClip (ALES10), two Clips heard together whose defining invariant is that LEFT and RIGHT hold the SAME length. This rung carries
//! the half-wave into stereo the plain way: run ALES85's proven mono `halve` on each channel over the SAME span, so one threshold at zero lands on BOTH speakers together.
//!
//! The half-wave PARTITIONS where the full-wave (ALES138) COLLAPSED. The full-wave folded +a and −a both onto a, so an out-of-phase master returned IDENTICAL; the half-wave keeps only the positive half, so an
//! out-of-phase master (right the exact negation of left) returns PARTITIONED — at each index exactly one channel survives: where left is positive it is kept while right (= −left, negative) goes to silence, and
//! where left is negative it goes to silence while right (positive) is kept. Two facts hold at every index: left[i] · right[i] = 0 (the channels are DISJOINT, never both sounding at once), and left[i] + right[i] =
//! |left[i]| (the two half-waves PARTITION the antisymmetric pair, their sum reconstructing the full-wave magnitude). That is the honest half-wave story read in the stereo field — the pair split into complementary
//! one-sided channels rather than folded to mono. Yet a 1:2 master keeps its ratio on the kept samples (both zero elsewhere), and an identical-channel master stays identical.
//!
//! The rule, stated once: max(x, 0) is EXACT for EVERY i16 with NO rail edge at all (a non-negative sample passes unchanged, a negative becomes 0 — neither branch can overflow), so the half-wave needs NO saturate —
//! even plainer than the full-wave lift. It carries no gain, no ceiling, and no threshold (fixed at zero), naming exactly one fault. Refuse BadRange (a span outside the samples) BEFORE either channel is mutated — the
//! check depends only on the shared length, made ONCE up front, so a refusal never rectifies one channel and leaves the other signed; otherwise halve both channels over [start, start+count) via ALES85's `halve`.
//! HalveError reused whole — the stereo lift adds no fault.
//!
//! The crux — four laws. THE STEREO HALVE LAW: each channel equals ALES85's mono `halve` over the same span byte for byte — every output max(x, 0), non-negatives passing unchanged and negatives going to silence, no
//! rail edge. THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW: left.len == right.len after the edit and both hold their starting length (a halve writes values only). THE IMAGE-PARTITION LAW: because max(x, 0) keeps only the positive half,
//! an out-of-phase master (right = −left) returns PARTITIONED — left[i] · right[i] = 0 (disjoint) and left[i] + right[i] = |left[i]| (the two half-waves summing to the full-wave magnitude) at every index; yet a 1:2
//! master keeps its ratio on the kept samples, an identical-channel master stays identical, every output non-negative on both, and the map is idempotent (twice equals once). THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: the lone
//! refusal (BadRange) leaves BOTH channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced; count = 0 the identity on both.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES139.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-040747_fill-ales139-lotus-stereo-half-wave-rectifier.md
//! Stands on: lotus/halve.rye (ALES85) — the mono half-wave rectifier run once per channel, max(x, 0) with no rail edge; lotus/stereo_rectify.rye (ALES138) — the pure-even full-wave sibling this rung partitions
//! where that one collapsed; lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) — the StereoClip (two mono Clips, left/right) and its balance invariant; lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) — the Clip, its buf/len/samples, sample_min/max.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL. Two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right) on one bench, siloed to lotus/. It changes sample VALUES only through ALES85's own halve, each output at most its input and
//! never below zero, never a length; the shape is a half-wave threshold at zero, instantaneous (no attack/release, no anti-aliasing). No saturate is owed — max(x, 0) never leaves the rail. No real sample rate, no
//! network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/stereo_halve.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const pan = @import("pan.rye");
const halve = @import("halve.rye");

pub const stereo_halve_version = "20260815.040747";

/// Stereo halve's faults are exactly ALES85's own `HalveError` — `BadRange` (a span outside the samples) — reused by name, since the stereo lift adds no new fault: it only runs the same halve on each channel after
/// one shared validation. The half-wave rectifier names no other fault (max(x, 0) has no illegal parameter — no gain, no ceiling, and the threshold is fixed at zero).
pub const StereoHalveError = halve.HalveError;

/// Half-wave-rectify `[start, start+count)` in BOTH channels of `sc`, the SAME threshold at zero on each, running ALES85's proven mono `halve`. Each sample becomes `max(x, 0)` — the positive half of the wave kept
/// whole, the negative half folded away to silence. Exact for every i16 with no rail edge (a non-negative sample passes unchanged, a negative becomes 0), so no saturate is owed. Validates the span once against the
/// shared length before either channel is mutated: refuses `BadRange`, both channels untouched. Values only — never the length; the two channels leave balanced and both non-negative over the span.
pub fn stereo_halve(sc: *pan.StereoClip, start: u32, count: u32) StereoHalveError!void {
    // invariant: the two channels enter balanced — the defining StereoClip invariant, the ground the edit preserves.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);

    // invariant: the span is legal — refused by name before any write, so a refusal never rectifies one channel and leaves the other signed. The span check depends only on the shared length, made once up front; it
    // mirrors ALES85's own guard exactly, so each mono halve below is pre-validated to succeed. The half-wave carries no other parameter, so this lone check is the whole gate.
    const len: u32 = sc.left.len;
    if (start > len) return error.BadRange;
    if (count > len - start) return error.BadRange;

    // Every check passed and holds identically for both channels, so each mono halve is pre-validated to succeed — no refusal can land between the two channel edits. The SAME threshold at zero on each.
    try halve.halve(&sc.left, start, count);
    try halve.halve(&sc.right, start, count);

    // invariant: the channels leave balanced — a halve writes values only, so each channel holds its starting length and the stereo image stays aligned in time.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
    assert(sc.left.len <= sc.left.buf.len and sc.right.len <= sc.right.buf.len);
}

// --- selftest: THE STEREO HALVE LAW — each channel equals ALES85's mono halve over the same span byte for byte; THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW — left.len == right.len after, both holding their starting length;
//     THE IMAGE-PARTITION LAW — an OUT-OF-PHASE master (right = −left) returns PARTITIONED (left·right = 0 and left+right = |left| at each index, the two half-waves splitting the pair), yet a 1:2 master keeps its
//     ratio on the kept samples, an identical-channel master stays identical, every output NON-NEGATIVE on both, the map idempotent; THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW — a refusal leaves both channels untouched and
//     balanced, count = 0 the identity ----

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_stereo(sc: *pan.StereoClip, l: []const i16, r: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(&sc.left, 0, l);
    try timeline.splice(&sc.right, 0, r);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. THE STEREO HALVE LAW — each channel equals ALES85's mono halve over the same span, proven side by side with different per-channel samples; non-negatives pass unchanged, negatives go to silence, and there
    //    is no rail edge (sample_min, the full-wave's one over-rail sample, simply becomes 0 here).
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ -300, 300, timeline.sample_min, 2000 }, &[_]i16{ 100, -50, -32767, -2500 });
        try stereo_halve(&sc, 0, 4);

        var ml = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&ml, 0, &[_]i16{ -300, 300, timeline.sample_min, 2000 });
        try halve.halve(&ml, 0, 4);
        var mr = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mr, 0, &[_]i16{ 100, -50, -32767, -2500 });
        try halve.halve(&mr, 0, 4);

        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), ml.samples())) return error.LeftChannelWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), mr.samples())) return error.RightChannelWrong;
        // by hand: max(−300,0)=0, max(300,0)=300, max(−32768,0)=0 (no rail edge), max(2000,0)=2000 / max(100,0)=100, max(−50,0)=0, max(−32767,0)=0, max(−2500,0)=0.
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 0, 300, 0, 2000 })) return error.LeftHandWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 100, 0, 0, 0 })) return error.RightHandWrong;
    }

    // 2. THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW — the channels stay equal length and each holds its starting length; a halve writes values only.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 100, -200, 300, -400, 500 }, &[_]i16{ -500, 400, -300, 200, -100 });
        try stereo_halve(&sc, 0, 5);
        if (sc.left.len != sc.right.len) return error.Unbalanced;
        if (sc.left.len != 5) return error.LengthChanged;
    }

    // 3a. THE IMAGE-PARTITION LAW — an OUT-OF-PHASE master (right the exact negation of left) rectified through the same threshold returns PARTITIONED: at each index exactly one channel survives, because a sample
    //     and its negation cannot both be positive. left[i] · right[i] = 0 (disjoint) and left[i] + right[i] = |left[i]| (the two half-waves summing to the full-wave magnitude). This is the honest CONTRAST with
    //     ALES138's full-wave, which collapsed the same pair to identical channels.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 100, -300, 500, -700 }, &[_]i16{ -100, 300, -500, 700 }); // right = −left, out of phase
        try stereo_halve(&sc, 0, 4);
        const l = sc.left.samples();
        const r = sc.right.samples();
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < 4) : (i += 1) {
            if (@as(i32, l[i]) * @as(i32, r[i]) != 0) return error.NotDisjoint; // never both sounding at once
        }
        // the two half-waves partition: left + right = |left| at each index (the original left magnitudes were 100,300,500,700).
        const mag = [_]i32{ 100, 300, 500, 700 };
        i = 0;
        while (i < 4) : (i += 1) {
            if (@as(i32, l[i]) + @as(i32, r[i]) != mag[i]) return error.NotPartition;
        }
        // by hand: left keeps its positives {100,0,500,0}, right keeps its own positives {0,300,0,700}.
        if (!eq(l, &[_]i16{ 100, 0, 500, 0 })) return error.LeftPartitionWrong;
        if (!eq(r, &[_]i16{ 0, 300, 0, 700 })) return error.RightPartitionWrong;
    }

    // 3b. THE RATIO HOLDS ON THE KEPT SAMPLES — a 1:2 master (right twice left) keeps its 1:2 ratio where left is positive (both kept, right = 2·left), and both go to silence where left is negative; the ratio
    //     survives on the surviving half.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 1000, -400, 700, -200 }, &[_]i16{ 2000, -800, 1400, -400 }); // right = 2·left
        try stereo_halve(&sc, 0, 4);
        const l = sc.left.samples();
        const r = sc.right.samples();
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < 4) : (i += 1) {
            if (@as(i32, r[i]) != 2 * @as(i32, l[i])) return error.RatioBroke; // kept: r = 2l; silenced: 0 = 2·0
        }
        if (!eq(l, &[_]i16{ 1000, 0, 700, 0 })) return error.RatioLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(r, &[_]i16{ 2000, 0, 1400, 0 })) return error.RatioRightWrong;
    }

    // 3c. EVERY OUTPUT NON-NEGATIVE ON BOTH, IDEMPOTENT, AND AN IDENTICAL-CHANNEL MASTER STAYS IDENTICAL — a half-wave rectifier makes a one-sided signal, so no output on either channel is below zero; halving twice
    //     equals once (a non-negative sample halves to itself); and a shared threshold on equal channels gives equal channels.
    {
        var once = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&once, &[_]i16{ 700, -1500, 4000, -90, 12 }, &[_]i16{ -4000, 900, -700, 33, -8 });
        try stereo_halve(&once, 0, 5);
        const l = once.left.samples();
        const r = once.right.samples();
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < 5) : (i += 1) {
            if (l[i] < 0 or r[i] < 0) return error.NegativeOutput;
        }
        var twice = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&twice, &[_]i16{ 700, -1500, 4000, -90, 12 }, &[_]i16{ -4000, 900, -700, 33, -8 });
        try stereo_halve(&twice, 0, 5);
        try stereo_halve(&twice, 0, 5);
        if (!eq(once.left.samples(), twice.left.samples())) return error.LeftNotIdempotent;
        if (!eq(once.right.samples(), twice.right.samples())) return error.RightNotIdempotent;

        var same = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&same, &[_]i16{ -500, 3000, -500, 3000 }, &[_]i16{ -500, 3000, -500, 3000 });
        try stereo_halve(&same, 0, 4);
        if (!eq(same.left.samples(), same.right.samples())) return error.IdentityChannelsSplit;
    }

    // 3d. THE SPAN DISCIPLINE — halving a sub-span leaves samples outside [start, count) exactly as they were on both channels, negatives among them preserved (the in-place discipline the whole suite keeps).
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ -100, -200, -500, 500, -300, -400 }, &[_]i16{ 400, 300, -500, 500, -300, -400 });
        try stereo_halve(&sc, 2, 2); // indices 2,3 only, both channels
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ -100, -200, 0, 500, -300, -400 })) return error.LeftSpanLeaked;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 400, 300, 0, 500, -300, -400 })) return error.RightSpanLeaked;
    }

    // 4a. THE ATOMICITY LAW, bad span — a span past the samples refuses BadRange, both channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced. The half-wave names no other fault (max(x, 0) has no illegal parameter),
    //     so this lone refusal is the whole atomicity story.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 5, -6, 7 }, &[_]i16{ -7, 6, -5 });
        if (stereo_halve(&sc, 1, 3)) |_| return error.BadRangeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongBadRange;
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 5, -6, 7 })) return error.BadRangeLeftMutated;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -7, 6, -5 })) return error.BadRangeRightMutated;
        if (sc.left.len != sc.right.len) return error.BadRangeUnbalanced;
    }

    // 4b. THE DEGENERATE LAW — count = 0 is the identity on both channels (an empty span thresholds nothing).
    {
        var noop = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&noop, &[_]i16{ 7, -8, 9 }, &[_]i16{ -7, 8, -9 });
        try stereo_halve(&noop, 1, 0); // empty span — identity
        if (!eq(noop.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 7, -8, 9 })) return error.ZeroCountLeftChanged;
        if (!eq(noop.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -7, 8, -9 })) return error.ZeroCountRightChanged;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-stereo-halve: the HALF-WAVE RECTIFIER CARRIED INTO STEREO, ONE SHARED THRESHOLD AT ZERO, the EVEN+ODD member of the stereo NONLINEAR class's even corner — half-wave-rectify a span in both " ++
        "channels of a master, running ALES85's proven mono halve on each (y = max(x, 0), the positive half kept whole and the negative folded away to silence, no rail edge and no saturate), so one threshold at " ++
        "zero lands on both speakers together. A Lotus master is a StereoClip (ALES10) whose defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length. The half-wave is the plain sibling of the full-wave " ++
        "rectifier (an even+odd mix, max(x,0) = (x+|x|)/2), and it PARTITIONS where the full-wave COLLAPSED: the full-wave folded an out-of-phase master to identical channels, but the half-wave keeps only the " ++
        "positive half, so an out-of-phase master (right = −left) returns PARTITIONED — at each index exactly one channel survives, left · right = 0 (disjoint) and left + right = |left| (the two half-waves " ++
        "summing to the full-wave magnitude); yet a 1:2 master keeps its ratio on the kept samples, an identical-channel master stays identical, every output on both channels is non-negative, and the map is " ++
        "idempotent (twice equals once). max(x, 0) is exact for every i16 with no rail edge, so no saturate is owed and this is the plainest lift in the class, naming exactly one fault: it validates the span once " ++
        "against the shared length before either channel is mutated (BadRange on a span past the samples), so a refusal never rectifies one channel and leaves the other signed; HalveError reused whole. The crux " ++
        "is four laws. THE STEREO HALVE LAW: each channel equals ALES85's mono halve over the same span byte for byte. THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW: left.len == right.len after and both hold their starting length. THE " ++
        "IMAGE-PARTITION LAW: an out-of-phase master returns partitioned (left · right = 0 and left + right = |left|), the ratio held on the kept samples, an identical master staying identical, every output " ++
        "non-negative, the map idempotent. THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: a refusal leaves both channels untouched and balanced, count = 0 the identity. Purely local — two bounded i16 Clips half-wave-rectified " ++
        "through the proven mono halve; no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
